    <p>The Willamette River Waterfront
        History and Development
        The Willamette River is the reason Portland exists. Founded in 1845 (decided by a literal coin toss), the city
        was originally a clearing in the woods known as "The Stumptown." Its location at the confluence of the
        Willamette and Columbia rivers made it the primary trade hub of the Pacific Northwest during the 19th century.

        1850s–1920s: The waterfront was a chaotic industrial zone of warehouses, docks, and mills.

        The Transformation: In the 1970s, Portland took a radical leap in urbanism by removing a major freeway (Harbor
        Drive) to create Governor Tom McCall Waterfront Park. This shifted the city’s identity from a place that used
        the water for industry to a place that lives with the water for recreation and community health.
</p>

    <p>What the Water Means to Me
        As I embark on my first West Coast chapter, the water represents connectivity and transition. In my professional
        life, I manage "flows" of data and commerce; seeing how the Willamette flows through the heart of Portland
        reminds me that infrastructure must serve the people, not just the economy. To me, this river is a symbol of
        Portland’s resilience—the ability to reclaim industrial space for public "livability," which is exactly what I
        hope to study in CityLab.</p>

